\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Ends Tad Stronger, Aided By Ctrl Bk Auction \b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2001\par 22:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- On a Friday of expiring dollar futures, local banks opened fire against Brazil's embattled real after a one-day truce Thursday, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contain the sell-off. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real closed slightly stronger at BRR2.671 to the dollar, following a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move to call an emergency sale of BRR500 million in May 2002 dollar-indexed notes. The auction was announced as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit its weakest point of the day at BRR2.690 in late trading. \par \par \uc2  The dollar-linked notes sold for an average rate of 6.36% \par \par \uc2  "Today it was pure speculation because of the expiring futures contracts," said a Sao Paulo-based trader. \par \par \uc2  He noted the late-afternoon weakening wasn't caused by corporate demand for hedge against \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation, as was the case in recent days. \par \par \uc2  The real strengthened 6.3 centavos Thursday to BRR2.675 to the dollar, reacting to a series of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to tighten liquidity and a market regulator ruling allowing companies to dilute \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses over the next four years. \par \par \uc2  The rally kept the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since last Friday. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real came under added strain after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks on the U.S. raised fear that foreign capital flows into emerging economies will fall significantly. \par \par \uc2  The real, which was already weak amid a host of regional and homegrown worries, lost 4% of its value since the U.S tragedy despite a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  move to flood the market with $5.74 billion in short-term dollar-indexed notes in the period. \par \par \uc2  The year-to-date depreciation is 27%. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said earlier Friday Brazil would draw down a $4.7 billion aid tranche from the International Monetary Fund to boost its international reserves, but the news didn't move the real. \par \par \uc2  The tranche is part of a recently-inked $15.5 billion loan program set to expire in December. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's international reserves were at $35.291 billion Thursday, down from $35.395 billion Wednesday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the $4.7 billion IMF tranche will add to reserves today. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Web Site: ww.bcb.gov.br \par \par \uc2  By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11 3145-1481; adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010928dx9s0019f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
