\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil In Spotlight As Volatile Week Ends In Latin Mkts \b0\par\par\uc2 685 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 23:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- A turbulent week for Latin American financial markets drew to a close with the spotlight on \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to keep the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from weakening to an all-time low. \par \par \uc2  The real fell 2.6% to BRR2.835 per dollar Friday, despite five impromptu \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions - in which more than BRR2 billion in dollar-linked debt was sold - aimed at stabilizing a market fearful of the economic fallout from last week's terrorist attacks in the U.S. A sixth auction was called after the close, a move which could ease pressure on the real early Monday. \par \par \uc2  The real weakened almost 6% during the week and has lost nearly a third of its value year-to-date. Traders pointed to heavy corporate demand for hedge. \par \par \uc2  "With Brazil, it's definitely a question of `who-would-have-thunk it?," said Daniel Tillotson, head emerging markets strategist at Prudential Securities in New York, noting that the market is being driven by psychological rather than fundamental considerations. \par \par \uc2  FleetBoston said Friday in a report that it had thought BRR2.75 per dollar was the "absolute top" for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if economically-troubled Argentina were forced to devalue. \par \par \uc2  "We think anything above 2.60 is overdone, but we also think 3.00 could trade next week based on the fear in the market that comes from reaching a new reference along with the fear from the external environment," the report added. \par \par \uc2  The anxiety was evident in New York-based debt trading Friday. Yield spreads widened 50 points to 1150 over comparable U.S. Treasurys, or about 400 points wider than at the end of 2000. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par           Mexican Peso Holds Ground\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Brazilian equities faired relatively better, taking their cue from the\par see-saw performance of U.S. stocks throughout the day.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The main Sao Paulo IBovespa index shed 1.2% Friday to 10,419, but\par still managed to gain 3.8% for the week. The Sao Paulo bourse suffered\par most of its losses last week, while U.S. markets were closed in the wake\par of the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In New York, Brazilian blue chips were mixed, with aircraft\par manufacturer Embraer (EMJ) gaining 2.9% to $12.30 - after having lost\par half of its value the previous four sessions -  and wireless phone\par service provider Telesp Celular (TCP) sliding 4.8% to $4.95, pushing its\par cumulative loss for the week to 33%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading activity elsewhere in the region Friday was more subdued,\par though strategists say volatility is bound to continue as the markets\par await details of U.S. plans for retaliation against the attacks and more\par information on the extent to which the global economy will suffer.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Mexico, the peso weakened slightly to MXN9.465 per dollar from\par MXN9.462, after plunging to around MXN9.53 early in the session on the\par heels of a sharp downturn on Wall Street. The peso gained nearly seven\par centavos during the week, recovering from a 20 centavo weakening the\par previous week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mexico's main IPC stock index, for its part, inched up 0.1% Friday to\par 5087.94. The index showed a cumulative 8% loss for the week, as trading\par resumed Monday for the first time since the U.S. tragedy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In Argentina, the thinly-traded equities market dipped 1.1% Friday,\par with the Merval index ending at 239.50 points, or 10% lower than at the\par end of the previous week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market participants are concerned that Argentina, like Brazil, could\par suffer the most from heightened investor risk aversion in the wake of\par last week's attacks, drying up much needed sources of funding.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The performance of Argentine debt indicates risk premiums are indeed\par on the rise. Yield spreads on Argentine debt were 1,637 points above\par Treasurys on Friday, hovering at their highest levels in a month. The\par benchmark FRB has fallen to around 71 1/2 bid from 75 bid the day before\par the attacks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Stephen Wisnefski, Dow Jones Newswires; (201) 938-5692;\par stephen.wisnefski@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010921dx9l001aa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
