\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Real Opens -2: Emerging Mkts Seen Suffering Most \b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2001\par 15:33\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Gravity yet again got the better of Brazil's real Friday morning amid worry over international conflict following terrorist attacks on the U.S. \par \par \uc2  By 1253 GMT, the real was trading at a midrate of BRR2.710 on a spread of BRR2.709 to BRR2.711 after closing stronger Thursday at BRR2.66 on the back of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  called an emergency auction of short-term dollar-indexed bills Thursday afternoon to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp plunge in the wake of Tuesday's terrorist attacks in the U.S. The real set an all-time intraday low of BRR2.740 Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The market fears that a possible international conflict will reduce capital flows to a trickle, hitting emerging markets the hardest. \par \par \uc2  "As the world fell into recession in recent months, several crunch points turned into crises," Bear Stearns said in its morning report. "These will worsen. In particular, Turkey, Argentina and Brazil are disproportionately harmed because they depend on risk tolerance." \par \par \uc2  Highlighting this point, the real has lost more than 10 centavos since Monday's close, while the main stocks index has plunged nearly 13%. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; anthony.dovkants@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010914dx9e000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
