\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Ctrl Bk To Continue  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Mkt  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  - Paper \b0\par\par\uc2 347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2001\par 12:03\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  PARIS -(Dow Jones)- The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at least until the end of this year, selling $50 million a day, in order to guarantee liquidity on the market, Luiz Fernando Figueiredo, head of the bank's monetary policy, said Tuesday in an interview with French newspaper Agefi. \par \par \uc2  "We have decided to sell $50 million a day, whatever the market conditions are. This strategy has to be continued until the end of the year and will be extended if necessary," Figueiredo said. \par \par \uc2  But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no target for the real's exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  Despite repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since June, the real has lost about 30% of its value this year and is currently trading just above its historic lows. \par \par \uc2  Monday, the real hit a historic intraday low of BRR2.610 to the dollar, before settling at BRR2.606. \par \par \uc2  In order to maintain its inflation target of 4% in 2001, over the summer the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates to 19%. \par \par \uc2  Figueiredo said this level could still change. \par \par \uc2  "This valuation is subject to information concerning the economic health of the country and could be modified in case of improvement or degradation of the situation," Figueiredo said. \par \par \uc2  He said the real's depreciation limited the fall in external demand, and said direct foreign investment could increase to $25 billion in 2002, from a projected $20 billion in 2001. \par \par \uc2  Figueiredo said financing of external debt "isn't a problem" for Brazil. \par \par \uc2  He said the country meets International Monetary Fund requirements and expressed optimism about the budget surplus target of 3.4% of gross domestic product this year and 3.5% in 2002. \par \par \uc2  "The energy rationing will weigh on GDP but will have only an indirect and limited impact on the public finances and won't stop the government reaching its budget targets," Figueiredo said. \par \par \uc2  Newspaper Web site {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.agefi.fr/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.agefi.fr}}}\par \par \uc2  -By Mirela Vlad, Dow Jones Newswires; 33 1 40 17 17 40 Mirela.Vlad@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010911dx9b003eb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
