\par\par \b \uc2  IMF To Give  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  More Room To  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  On  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  -Source \b0\par\par\uc2  By Adriana Arai \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 585 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 August 2001\par 22:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- The International Monetary Fund has agreed to give Brazil more freedom to use its hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend its sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed, a top Brazilian government official told Dow Jones Newswires Monday. \par \par \uc2  The official, who is directly involved in Brazil's talks with the IMF and spoke on condition of anonymity, confirmed talk that South America's biggest economy will be allowed to use up to an extra $5 billion of its international reserves to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  feels it's necessary. \par \par \uc2  The IMF said late Friday it's preparing a fresh $15 billion loan program for Brazil in a bid to halt contagion from cash-strapped neighbor Argentina, whose financial woes have sent Brazilian markets reeling in recent months. Contagion fears are largely blamed for the real's 20%-plus depreciation since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Brazil currently has about $35.5 billion in hard-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, including $3.8 billion in IMF funds. Under an existing $41.5 billion IMF aid program that will be replaced in September, the country is committed to keeping reserves above $25 billion. \par \par \uc2  That reserve floor will be lowered to some $20 billion, the official said. \par \par \uc2  The Finance Ministry said it's set to unveil the details of the new aid package Tuesday at 1730 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier said it will sell $6 billion in dollars on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the end of the year, by means of daily sales worth about $50 million. The move aims to bridge a lack of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market amid regional turbulence. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  is hoping that the extra $5 billion freed for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  purposes will help ward off speculative pressure on the real. \par \par \uc2  A strategy in late June to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on the market failed, though. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pumped $1.3 billion in dollars into the market during a two-week period, but the real's recovery was very short-lived. \par \par \uc2  The government official also confirmed that the IMF demanded that Brazil raise its primary budget surplus target - which excludes debt servicing costs - this year and next in order to keep Brazil's net public debt burden of some 50% of GDP from growing further. \par \par \uc2  This year's surplus target will be raised to 3.4% of gross domestic product and next year's to around 3.5%, the source said. The goals had been set at 3%. \par \par \uc2  The official said no more budget cuts will be needed this year to meet the new 2001 target after the government last month unveiled spending cuts totaling 1 billion reals ($1=BRR2.46). \par \par \uc2  Some of next year's investments will likely have to be sacrificed, though. \par \par \uc2  "We're going to work on the budget, and the 2002 budget proposal that we're presenting to Congress on August 30 will show how we'll manage to meet the target," said the official. \par \par \uc2  He said new tax hikes, which made up the bulk of the government's austerity measures in recent years, won't be necessary this time around. \par \par \uc2  An energy crisis and Argentine turmoil led economists to downgrade their forecasts for Brazil's growth this year to 2% to 3%, down from earlier projections of a 4% to 4.5% expansion. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11 3145-1481 adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010806dx8600166\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
