\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Real Ends Stronger On Ctrl Bk Dlr Sale, Argentina \b0\par\par\uc2 434 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2001\par 22:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real finished stronger Monday, thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Argentine government winning approval for a plan to cut spending by $1.5 billion in the second half of this year. \par \par \uc2  The real finished a midrate of BRR2.415 to the dollar on a spread of BRR2.414 to BRR2.416. The real closed Friday at BRR2.460 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was encouraged by the opposition-led Senate approving the Argentine government's bill but added it continued to remain skeptical about the country being able to emerge from its three-year recession. \par \par \uc2  This doubt crept into the real's price during midsession after the initial lift in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early trading, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sold dollars to prop up the real. \par \par \uc2  The latest Argentine update comes as press reports circulated in the local market that Brazil has asked the International Monetary Fund to allow it to have the flexibility to lower the minimum amount it has to hold in its reserves. \par \par \uc2  According to daily newspaper Folha de S. Paulo, the Brazilian government would like to have more firepower to prop up the beleaguered real, which has depreciated about 19% so far this year. \par \par \uc2  The current agreement allows Brazil to lower its reserve levels to a minimum of $25 billion. Reserves are currently worth about $35.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  The request comes during talks with the IMF about the possibility of setting up a new accord. According to the latest press speculation, a new accord could be worth between $10 billion and $20 billion. Talks between Brazil and the IMF began in Washington last week. If put together, a new accord would succeed an existing $41.5 billion IMF-led bailout package that was brokered in late 1998. \par \par \uc2  Economists say Brazil needs a lifeline from the IMF because recent regional economic shocks threaten to produce a vicious cycle in which rising interest rates, a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and slowing economic growth aggravate the country's already heavy debt burden. \par \par \uc2  Also, Brazil's commercial and retail users have to cut electricity use by an average 20% a month between June and December to preserve depleted water-reservoir levels in a country that is largely dependent on hydroelectric power. \par \par \uc2  Economists believe the power crunch could shave one to two percentage points off of this year's initially forecast 4.0% to 4.5% economic growth rate. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; anthony.dovkants@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010730dx7u0025t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
