\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Ends Stronger;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again \b0\par\par\uc2 216 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2001\par 23:07\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real finished stronger Thursday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars to prop up its beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The real closed at a midrate of BRR2.480 to the dollar on a spread of BRR2.479 to BRR2.481. Wednesday, the real finished at a midrate of BRR2.487, weaker compared with Tuesday, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Thursday's improved finish comes despite growing concern about the Argentine government's bid to try and get its so-called zero-deficit plan approved by the Senate. \par \par \uc2  Traders said they continued to worry over news that Argentine lawmakers had postponed a vote on a key plan to cut government spending. In addition, the market was concerned about whether the opposition party wants to introduce changes to the bill in a move that would require the bill to return to the lower house. \par \par \uc2  The market is concerned because the new austerity plan, which seeks to cut government spending by $1.5 billion, plays a key role in Argentina's effort to alleviate pressures on its troubled economy. Markets are largely worried that Argentina could end up defaulting on its debt. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; anthony.dovkants@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020010726dx7q002xl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
