\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Ends Weaker Despite Ctrl Bk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 399 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juli 2001\par 23:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real finished weaker Wednesday even though the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. \par \par \uc2  The real closed at a midrate of BRR2.487 to the dollar on a spread of BRR2.486 to BRR2.488, pulled down by worry over troubled Argentina. Tuesday, the real finished at a midrate of BRR2.470 to the dollar, despite the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  being boosted by $545.7 million in receipts from a Petrobras share offering last week and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Wednesday, traders said comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary Paul O'Neill fueled market worry about Argentina after he voiced support for Brazil's new funding accord with the International Monetary Fund. \par \par \uc2  Traders interpreted the comment as a sign that Brazil needs to shore up its defenses against possible fallout from Argentine contagion. \par \par \uc2  The market was partly reassured, however, by Brazil's announcement that this year it will cut BRR1 billion from its budget, which will now total BRR51.2 billion. Traders said the market was also encouraged by the start of talks between the IMF and Brazil Wednesday morning. \par \par \uc2  According to latest press speculation, Brazil will ask for a six-month "standby" fund from the IMF and a loan of between $15 billion and $20 billion. The accord is expected to be in place once the current $41.5 billion IMF-led bailout package ends in December. \par \par \uc2  Economists say Brazil needs a lifeline from the IMF because recent regional economic shocks threaten to produce a vicious cycle in which rising interest rates, a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and slowing economic growth aggravate Brazil's already heavy debt burden. Argentina's fiscal crisis, marked by its recession and a $130 billion public-sector debt of its own, has also hurt Brazil. \par \par \uc2  What's more, Brazil's trade deficit is growing, and foreign investment, needed to finance this gap, is sharply down. Also, Brazil's commercial and retail electricity customers are having to cut usage by an average 20% a month between June to December to preserve depleted water reservoir levels in a country that's largely dependent on hydroelectric power. \par \par \uc2  Economists believe the power crunch could shave one to two percentage points off this year's initially forecast 4% to 4.5% economic growth rate. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; anthony.dovkants@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020010725dx7p00ffl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
