\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Real Ends Weaker On Argentina Deficit Bill Worry \b0\par\par\uc2 231 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juli 2001\par 22:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones Commodities Service \par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real closed weaker Tuesday on renewed concern about troubled Argentina. \par \par \uc2  The real finished at a midrate of BRR2.470 to the dollar on a spread of BRR2.469 to BRR2.471. Monday, the real closed at BRR2.415 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Initially, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was boosted by trickles of $545.7 million in receipts from a Petrobras share offering last week. Also, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped prop up the real by selling dollars. \par \par \uc2  However, market concerns over Argentina started to push the real lower by early afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the market was worried about possible delays or a clash between the Argentine government and the opposition-controlled Senate over approval of a so-called zero-deficit plan geared toward cutting $1.5 billion in spending by the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  While the government said it would be able to fast track the bill through the Senate, market concerns have mounted over whether the opposition will delay the move in protest of the budget-cutting plan. \par \par \uc2  The program is part of Argentine President Fernando de la Rua's latest effort to try to revive an economy that is mired in three years of recession. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; anthony.dovkants@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djcs000020010724dx7o00hqx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichg