\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  rallies on Argentina debt progress. \b0\par\par\uc2 492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2001\par 23:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, Brazil, July 17 (Reuters) - Brazil's battered real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rallied 3.3 percent against the dollar on Tuesday after the Argentine government won key political support for an austerity plan that could help avert a debt default, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Taking advantage of the optimistic mood and to secure a significant rebound in the flagging real, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Traders said the bank sold dollars, giving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  the stamina to extend its gains. \par \par \uc2  The real firmed for a second straight day to close at 2.50 reais per dollar from 2.582 reais at Monday's close. The real on Monday gained for the first time in five days after it hit consecutive record lows last week. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained even after ratings agency Fitch changed its outlook on Brazil's ratings to negative from stable, the first negative rating move on Brazil since January 1999. \par \par \uc2  "Our neighbors declared a truce and market players here gave us some respite," said Joao Marcos Cicarelli, a consultant at Agente brokerage in Sao Paulo. "It was the inverse domino effect of past days." \par \par \uc2  The respite came after the Argentine government gained the backing of powerful opposition Peronist governors for a program of spending cuts aimed at reducing the public deficit to zero. \par \par \uc2  The program is the government's latest attempt to assuage chronic investor anxiety of an Argentine default on its $128 billion in debt, and is seen as a prerequisite to dragging Latin America's No. 3 economy out of three-years of recession. \par \par \uc2  Argentine default fears have sparked capital flight from emerging market assets from Turkey to Russia and particularly from neighboring Brazil in what some have taken to calling the "Tango effect." \par \par \uc2  The real market has felt the Argentine crisis more acutely than other assets as it added to concerns about the impact of a U.S. economic slowdown and emergency energy rationing at home. \par \par \uc2  The real is 22 percent lower against the dollar than at the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  But Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been determined to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have become a regular event in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the bank said on July 5 that it would spend $6 billion in the market in regular doses this year to help support the real. \par \par \uc2  The bank has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost daily with $50 million cash injections. \par \par \uc2  Market players said they were also bracing themselves ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Meeting (Copom), which started on Tuesday and is expected to announce a fifth consecutive interest rate hike on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Seventeen of 22 economists polled by Reuters in the last two trading days said they expected a hike in the current 18.25 percent benchmark Selic rate, but forecasts ranged from 50 basis points to 200 basis points. The other five respondents expected the bank to hold rates steady. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010717dx7h013ib\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
