\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil real  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  firms on Argentina pact. \b0\par\par\uc2 395 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 2001\par 19:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, July 17 (Reuters) - Brazil's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened in Tuesday afternoon trading as \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales added to optimism that recession-hit Argentina was reducing the risk of debt default, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The real strengthened to 2.508 per dollar from Monday's close of 2.582 or about 22 percent weaker than where it began the year. Monday was the first time in five days the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmed after hitting consecutive record lows on fears of fallout from Argentina's economic woes. \par \par \uc2  Argentina's powerful opposition Peronist governors agreed late Monday to back the government's "zero deficit" plan, which would restrict the provincial governments to spend only what they receive in tax revenues. \par \par \uc2  Emerging market players across the world read the deal as a sign Latin America's No. 3 economy was making progress in reducing the risk of a dreaded debt default. \par \par \uc2  The fear Argentina may not make its debt payments has punished Latin American markets in recent months and has weighed on emerging markets from Turkey to Russia, in what some have taken to calling the "Tango effect." \par \par \uc2  The real market has felt the Argentine crisis more acutely than other assets as it added to concerns about the impact of a U.S. economic slowdown and emergency energy rationing at home. \par \par \uc2  "Apart from an interruption of the 'Tango effect,' at least for now, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the dollar slip," one trader said. Other dealers said they too had seen the bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have become a regular event in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the bank's said on July 5 that it would spend $6 billion in the market in regular doses this year to help support the real. It has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  almost daily with $50 million cash injections. \par \par \uc2  Market players said they were also bracing themselves ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Meeting (Copom), which starts on Tuesday and is expected to announce a fifth consecutive interest rate hike after the market close on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Seventeen of 22 economists polled by Reuters in the last two trading days said they expected a hike in the current 18.25 percent benchmark Selic rate, but forecasts ranged from 50 basis points to 200 basis points. The other five respondents expected the bank to hold rates steady. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010717dx7h00x12\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
