\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Ends Stronger For First Time In 5 Sessions \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juli 2001\par 22:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real Monday finished on a stronger note for the first time in five sessions despite continuing concern about troubled Argentina. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's real closed at a midrate of BRR2.581 on a spread of BRR2.580 to BRR2.582. Friday, the real ended at BRR2.590 to the dollar - its weakest closing point since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was first introduced in 1994 to stamp out hyperinflation. \par \par \uc2  The stronger finish Monday, however, comes after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was forced to rescue the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it breached the psychologically important level of BRR2.600 to the dollar for the first time ever. \par \par \uc2  At its weakest point, the real depreciated to as much as BRR2.606, making it the fourth session in a row that the real has set a record intraday low. \par \par \uc2  Traders said there was concern Monday that Argentine President Fernando de la Rua may not find the political support necessary to carry out a proposal to cut government spending by $1.5 billion through the end of 2001. This plan still awaits support from the majority of Argentina's political heavyweights. \par \par \uc2  The market is worried that Argentina is close to defaulting, as a number of analysts don't believe that Argentine Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo and de la Rua will be able to revive an economy mired in three years of recession. \par \par \uc2  Troubled Argentina has also been bogged down by rumors over the last week that de la Rua could resign. This was also coupled with speculation that Cavallo was planning to quit because of de la Rua's rising frustration at the economy minister's failure to spark economic growth. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; anthony.dovkants@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010716dx7g0021g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
