\par\par \b \uc2  Loser:IMF Is Confident Of Argentine Recovery- Estado \b0\par\par\uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2001\par 21:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- A top official of the International Monetary Fund reiterated that he's confident about President Fernando De la Rua and Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo's ability to lift Argentina out of a financial crisis, local news agency Agencia Estado reported Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Argentine securities have come under heavy selling in recent days amid fears that the government's lackluster revenues won't be enough to cover its debt obligations. \par \par \uc2  The government is now pushing through spending cuts in an attempt to stay solvent, while trying to end a three-year recession. \par \par \uc2  Speaking in Brasilia, Claudio Loser - the IMF's head for the Western Hemisphere - said he doesn't believe Argentina will declare a debt moratorium or devalue its peso, which is pegged 1-for-1 to the dollar, except for foreign trade transactions. \par \par \uc2  Loser added that the IMF isn't considering extending additional credit to Argentina. The two parties entered a loan agreement about six months ago. \par \par \uc2  Loser is in Brasilia to review Brazil's compliance with fiscal goals set by the IMF as part of a January 1999 bailout package. \par \par \uc2  The IMF put together a $41.5 billion loan to Brazil after a devaluation of the real, which had been pegged to the dollar for about five years. \par \par \uc2  So far, Loser has kept to the IMF's stance that the IMF is willing to talk about extending Brazil's existing IMF agreement in some form, but only if Brazil requested such assistance. \par \par \uc2  Loser said Brazil doesn't need additional IMF funds at this time to fend off contagion from Argentina. \par \par \uc2  In an interview earlier this week with a local newspaper, Brazilian President Fernando Henrique Cardoso didn't rule out extending the IMF accord, but said he doesn't think an extension is necessary at the moment. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have said it might be wise for Brazil - which has already seen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken over 20% since January amid Argentine trouble and an energy crisis - to obtain a contingent credit line, or CCL, from the IMF to use as an insurance policy. \par \par \uc2  Also, Loser denied that the IMF was talking with \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  about allowing the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the futures market for the real, Estado reported. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  frequently in the real's spot market in recent months to prop up the sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but entering the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures market is prohibited under the existing IMF accord, which expires in December. \par \par \uc2  If Brazil wanted to enter a new IMF agreement, it would have to wait until its existing program expires in December before closing such a deal, under normal IMF rules. \par \par \uc2  -Terry Wade, Dow Jones Newswires; 5511.3145.1479; terry.wade@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010712dx7c0013h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
