\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Brazil real hits new low on Argentine pandemonium. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nicholas Winning \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 2001\par 00:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, July 12 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's embattled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ignored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to chalk up its third record low close in a row on Thursday as players took a dim view of Argentina's latest bid to pull itself out of recessionary quagmire. \par \par \uc2  The real weakened to the fresh all-time low close of 2.552 per dollar, below Wednesday's previous historic low finish of 2.500, leaving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly 31 percent below where it began the year. \par \par \uc2  The real also hit a new record intraday low of 2.58 along the way as fears Latin America's No.3 economy could default sent the world's emerging markets reeling for the second day in a row despite Argentina's attempts to calm the storm. \par \par \uc2  Traders were skeptical that Argentine President Fernando de la Rua would be able to muster enough political support to make good on his vow late Wednesday to eliminate the budget gap for the rest of the year by cutting spending and tax evasion. \par \par \uc2  "The (Brazilian) market is 100 percent looking at Argentina," said Banif-Primus head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rodrigo Trotta. \par \par \uc2  As the real headed toward its record close President Fernando Henrique Cardoso and members of his cabinet gave upbeat speeches at an event to commemorate seven years of the real plan, the policy which gave birth to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Our public debt is going to increase...our face is still ugly, " Cardoso said. "But we are going to improve. We are not afraid of anything." \par \par \uc2  THREE REAIS TO THE DOLLAR? \par \par \uc2  Pressured the past months by concern over the U.S. economic slowdown and the impact of an acute energy shortage, the real's latest slide has made front page news and led to regular updates of its daily trajectory on television news shows. \par \par \uc2  One local newspaper said the real could sink to 3.0 per dollar. Economists do not rule that out but add it would be a knee-jerk reaction to an Argentine collapse and would not last as it would not reflect Brazil's economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  "It's not impossible that it could reach 3.0...but that would not be sustained," said BES Investimento chief economist Sandra Utsumi. "As long as there's no definition (for Argentina), the trend is for the dollar to keep pressuring." \par \par \uc2  As traders expected, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for the seventh time this month on Thursday. Its sale of $2.7 billion reais in NBC-E dollar-indexed notes to roll over debt due to expire on Monday also gave the real some support. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has placed a $50 million cushion into the market in each of the last six trading days in the hope of breaking the real's fall. \par \par \uc2  The bank, which has repeatedly voiced its well-worn mantra that it targets inflation and not the exchange rate, has said it will spend $6 billion in the local market this year. \par \par \uc2  But players fear that may not be enough and the bank may need to jack up interest rates to up to 30 percent if Argentina collapses, putting the brakes on economic growth which has already been slowed by energy rationing. \par \par \uc2  In a reflection of how worried investors have become, Brazil's risk rating, or the spread between its bond prices and safe-haven U.S. Treasuries, rose past 1,000 basis points, putting it near Turkey and beyond Russia. \par \par \uc2  BCP Latin American economist Walter Molano said Argentina had to bite the bullet and implement economically tough measures to put its economy back on track just as Mexico did after the so-called 1995 Tequila crisis. \par \par \uc2  "Otherwise Argentina will go into the abyss and we will all lose," he said in his daily report. "The contagion from an Argentine collapse will be real. The losses will be severe, and emerging markets will die as an asset class." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx7c020uh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
