\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2 /Ctrl Bk/Real -3: \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Confirms  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 205 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juli 2001\par 20:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2001, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Later, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, but didn't give more details about the amount of dollars sold. \par \par \uc2  The real was also cheered by comments from an International Monetary Fund official that there is no need for Brazil to renew its current agreement with the fund, traders said. \par \par \uc2  IMF director Claudio Loser said the IMF mission, which is currently in Brazil to review the $41.5 billion aid package, is satisfied that the country is complying with the agreement. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the real weakened to its lowest closing mark ever of BRR2.492 per dollar after Argentina sold $827.6 million of 91-day Treasury bills, or letes, at an annual yield of 14.01% - the highest rate for the security in recent years. \par \par \uc2  Comments by Argentina's Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo broadcast on Argentine television sent more shockwaves through local markets. \par \par \uc2  Cavallo said the country can't continue to pay high yields on debt. Traders said the market in Brazil interpreted Cavallo's comments as a warning that a default in Argentina is likely. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Brasileiro, Dow Jones Newswires; 5521-2580-9394, adriana.brasileiro@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx7b033r9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
