\par\par \b \uc2  Brazilian real drop prompts rate hike consideration. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Genevieve Wilkinson \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2001\par 19:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, July 6 (Reuters) - As Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbles and inflation gathers speed, Wall Street is mulling the possibility that the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be forced to raise interest rates to stem the tide of money flowing out of Latin America's biggest economy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been unable to prick the bubble in the real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - on Friday it hit a fresh all-time low, sinking 31 percent this year - despite a 150 basis point hike in the benchmark Selic interest rate and numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market using a $6 billion war chest. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, inflation targets were last week raised a full percentage point to 5.8 percent, propelled higher by an energy crisis and the real's weakness while flaccid U.S. growth threatens \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fiscal stability. \par \par \uc2  While repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  merely line the pocket of speculators - in the ranks of which \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga once belonged - economists say the bank's next move might be an interest rate hike, not a cut as indicated two weeks ago. \par \par \uc2  "It's fair to say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strategy hasn't worked as well as they would have liked," Graham Stock, Latin American sovereign debt strategist at J.P. Morgan, said of the bank's declaration of war to do what it takes to save the drowning real. \par \par \uc2  "They don't have too many tools, and if they are going to maintain their focus on the inflation target this year and next, then they will almost certainly need to raise rates at the next meeting or even call an extraordinary meeting before July 17." \par \par \uc2  The minutes of the June 20 meeting of the Monetary Policy Committee (Copom) showed the bank jacked up rates to combat persistent inflationary pressures and to head off the potential effect of an unstable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on consumer prices. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, interest rate futures markets have priced in higher Brazilian rates. On Thursday the 30-day interest rate future closed at 21.21 percent, up from 20.44 percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Selic rate stands at 18.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  Yet raising interest rates two weeks after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  adopted an easing bias - meaning it was more inclined to lower borrowing costs - might damage the bank's credibility, some economists say. \par \par \uc2  "If Argentina has stabilized, then raising interest rates would somewhat tarnish the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credibility," said Tulio Vera, emerging market strategist at Merrill Lynch. "But I don't think we're quite at the point where we can say raising interest rates is the most likely scenario ... I don't think the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will give up so easily." \par \par \uc2  ARGENTINE CONTAGION \par \par \uc2  What's most unfortunate for Brazil is that economic and political turmoil in Argentina has shot investors' confidence in much of Latin America, with trade partner Brazil taking the biggest hit. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday Brazil's benchmark sovereign bond, the C bond, touched 13-month lows as political cohesion in Argentina threatened to come undone, further complicating the government's efforts to end 10 straight quarters of economic contraction. \par \par \uc2  "If Argentina deteriorates and the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  views this as a near-term threat, then they certainly would raise interest rates," one New York based fund manager said, adding that Brazil could always request support from the International Monetary Fund or even the U.S. Treasury Department. \par \par \uc2  Yet if Brazil keeps raising rates, it risks choking off growth in the $450 billion economy that the government expects to grow 2.8 percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil should let the real find its natural level," one Miami-based fund manager said. "They could find themselves in a worse predicament if they keep raising interest rates. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  overreacts, it gives the market another target to shoot at." \par \par \uc2  Santiago Millan, Latin American economist at HSBC, said \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Arminio Fraga has not run out of ammunition in the battle to save the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  before Copom meets on July 17-18. \par \par \uc2  While the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday announced it was ready to deploy $6 billion by year's end to lift the sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Brazil can still use $11 billion more from its foreign reserves before broaching the IMF's prescribed $25 billion target. \par \par \uc2  "But the idea here is not to use it up too quickly - if there is a real crisis, you want to be prepared and have all your bullets," Millan said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010728dx7600ta1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
