\par\par \b \uc2  Man sees white sugar prices falling in medium term. \b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juli 2001\par 01:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, July 6 (Reuters) - Physical demand for white sugar is likely to slow in the future due to the high whites premium, but prices may subsequently slip as supply increases, British commodity house ED & F Man said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "We see white sugar values coming off in the medium term as fresh supplies from Brazil and possibly India and the EU come to the market," Man said. \par \par \uc2  In its latest monthly sugar report, Man said sugar supplies from EU producers continued to be priced at a premium to the terminal market. \par \par \uc2  "The upward move in the physical differential value indicates a break-out from the oversupply situation seen earlier in the year," the report stated. \par \par \uc2  The London daily sugar price (LDP) rose from $10 to $15 over the nearby August contract during the past month, reflecting nearby tightness in deliverable EU supplies. \par \par \uc2  But Man said other origins had not been able to follow the EU differential higher. \par \par \uc2  "Physical differentials for low-quality sugars from other origins have declined in reaction to a subdued demand situation," it said. \par \par \uc2  Brazilian 150 ICUMSA sugar fell from $12 under the term to a $25 discount to the August contract at the end of May for July shipment onward \par \par \uc2  Thai premiums fell to $5 over August, having remained at around $16 over for most of the year. \par \par \uc2  Raw sugar supplies from Brazil were seen continuing to be abundant in the near future, partly due to the real devaluation. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the real suffered its worst one-day percentage loss in over two years and \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been forced to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the country's sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Brazilian shipments from the main Centre-South growing region during May were far in excess of last year's tonnage, Man said, with more than one million tonnes expected to be shipped up to the middle of June. \par \par \uc2  It said current expectations for the southern Brazil crop (May/April) were for an export availability of around 5.5 million tonnes, but may be subject to revision depending on alcohol consumption. \par \par \uc2  "A range of estimates for the current cane crop has so far been released, the crucial number seems to be the alcohol consumption figure," according to the report. \par \par \uc2  Man said it was maintaining its estimate of 12 billion litres of alcohol, but warned it may have to be lowered if the Brazilian economy was affected by the Argentine financial and economic turmoil. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010912dx75003rl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
