\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  slips as jitters sweep markets. \b0\par\par\uc2 293 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2001\par 16:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, June 26 (Reuters) - Brazil's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped against the dollar in early trading on Tuesday as jittery markets on Wall Street and in Europe prompted investors to withdraw some capital from Brazilian assets, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The embattled real, which has been whipped to record lows in past weeks, weakened to 2.314 reais per dollar after strengthening on Monday for a fourth straight day to close at 2.30 reais. \par \par \uc2  "The market opened without definition. The predominant trend is the rough times we are seeing in foreign markets," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. \par \par \uc2  Stock indices in Europe and the United States dropped amid jitters over corporate earnings in an environment of slower growth. That unnerved emerging market investors who often seek out safer havens for their assets at times of global volatility. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's benchmark Bovespa stock index was off 0.6 percent by mid-morning. \par \par \uc2  Traders said the renewed slide in \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could prompt the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling dollars to banks. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice since Thursday of last week. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action last week helped pull the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back from an all-time low when it was trading 27 percent weaker than at the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  The bank hiked interest rates by 150 basis points and said it was building a war chest of $10.8 billion to bolster its foreign reserves and buttress the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  when need be. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been rocked this year by nervousness over a financial crisis in neighboring Argentina and more recently, by Brazil's chronic energy shortage that is forecast to cut growth and nudge up inflation levels. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6q00tyv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
