\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging Markets Debt Up As Brazil's Real Strengthens \b0\par\par\uc2 502 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Juni 2001\par 23:38\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Emerging markets debt prices were rising slightly in thin trading late Monday as Brazil's strengthened \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  cheered participants. \par \par \uc2  Most investors were avoiding placing major bets before the U.S. Federal Reserve meets on Wednesday, according to traders. Still, in a sign that market sentiment is improving, spreads on the J.P. Morgan Emerging Markets Bond Index Plus, or EMBI+, were tightening around 9 basis points to 738 basis points over comparable U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  The Brazilian real opened 1.2% stronger against the dollar Monday, extending its rally into a third straight session and reversing declines that sent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record lows earlier this month. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked interest rates last Wednesday and has vowed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much as necessary to shore up the real, which in turn has shored up investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's Capitalization bond was climbing around 1/2 a point to 76 1/8 bid, amid reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  was enjoying its positive thunder for the day, Argentine Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo on Monday had his own words about his country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , calling the present and future value of the peso "the best in the world" at a conferencein Buenos Aires. He predicted his country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will strengthen against the dollar and that lending rates in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fall below those on dollar- and euro-denominated credits. \par \par \uc2  While investors might not be as optimistic, they also seemed less worried than last week, when they dumped Argentine debt in the wake of a surprise devaluation of the peso for foreign trade transactions. Economists are estimating Argentina can place debt at its biweekly local Treasury bill auction Tuesday to yield around 9.0% - well below the 12% the government was forced to pay earlier in the year when fears grew it wouldn't be able to make its debt payments. \par \par \uc2  Near closing time Argentina's Floating Rate Bond was rising around 9/16 of a point to 85 1/8 bid. \par \par \uc2  Bigger bets will likely have to wait until after the Fed rules on interest rates Wednesday, though. Investors expect the Fed to cut rates 25 or 50 basis points - the more substantial the cut, the greater the anticipated lift for emerging markets. \par \par \uc2  Mexican debt prices improved slightly less than Brazilian and Argentine paper, tightening around 6 basis points on the day on the EMBI+. \par \par \uc2  Morgan Stanley raised its debt recommendation on Mexico to "outperform," citing the likelihood of an investment grade rating from Standard & Poor's before the year is out. \par \par \uc2  But investors might be in for a bit of a wait. \par \par \uc2  "People are saying the upgrade won't happen until late summer or early fall," a trader said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moody's bestowed an investment grade rating on Mexico in March 2000.\par    -By Sonja Ryst, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2245;\par sonja.ryst@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1179 1810 4071 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010710dx6p00s76\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
