\par\par \b \uc2  Santander Suggests Move Out Of Venezuela Debt Into Brazil \b0\par\par\uc2 430 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 2001\par 17:50\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Santander Central Hispano Investment on Friday recommended that investors switch out of Venezuelan debt and into Brazil paper following the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent announcement of measures to shore up the real and boost international reserves. \par \par \uc2  "Venezuela already looked toppish at current levels with little positive news to sustain asset prices' upward trend, aside from the potential for a push from the broader market," Santander said in a report, noting that Venezuela was the only Latin America credit to lose ground Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "Quite the contrary, Brazil could steal some of this momentum from Venezuela with new (Brazilian) \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures - for the time being at least," Santander added. \par \par \uc2  Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Wednesday hiked interest rates 150 basis points to 18.25% to keep the real, which had lost more than 20% of its value year to date, from tumbling further. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did, however, established an easing policy bias. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness had sparked concerns about the outlook for inflation and fiscal stability. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced measures to boost the country's international reserves by $6.2 billion by year's end, through the issue of an additional $1.4 billion in sovereign debt and the sale of certain state assets. Brazil also plans to draw down $2 billion in International Monetary Fund aid. \par \par \uc2  "Brazil's plans to increase international reserves should give Brazilian assets renewed vigor - at least as long as they appear affective in boosting the value of the real," Santander said. \par \par \uc2  Early Friday, the real strengthened to BRR2.337 per dollar compared to Thursday's closing mark of BRR2.381. The real had tested the BRR2.50 per dollar earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  Until this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had relied on a gradual cycle of monetary tightening and occasional \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to address a deteriorating economic scenario. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prospects have sourced this year amid economic turmoil in neighboring Argentina, local political jitters and an energy crisis. \par \par \uc2  Santander said that the surprisingly large increase in Brazil's benchmark interest rate, coupled with the easing bias, "sends a front-loaded, credible signal of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  intentions to stop the slide of the real and meet inflation targets." \par \par \uc2  "Minimizing uncertainty about the extent and duration of future monetary tightening should be especially positive at the short end (of the debt curve) where assets ...have experienced unusually high volatility relative to longer assets," the report said. \par \par \uc2  -By Stephen Wisnefski, Dow Jones Newswires; (201) 938-5692; stephen.wisnefski@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6m02u0t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
