\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  says acted in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market. \b0\par\par\uc2 195 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2001\par 22:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO, June 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Thursday that it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second time this week after traders said the Bank had sold dollars to boost the beleaguered Brazilian real BRBY). \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not specify how active it was in the market. The Bank has a policy of only commenting on its market activities after the close and not specifying how or when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Traders said they had noticed the Bank had sold dollars after a real rally, caused by the Bank's announcement that it would put together a $10.8 billion war chest to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , petered out. \par \par \uc2  The real firmed 3.5 percent to close at 2.381 per dollar from Wednesday's close of 2.468 per dollar, but remained about 22 percent below where it was in January. \par \par \uc2  The real has lost value since January on concerns about Argentine and U.S. economic weakness and political scandals and energy rationing at home. The Bank said earlier it had sold $455 million on Monday to support the real. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx6l00zgp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
