\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil To Draw Down $2 Bln In IMF Funds - Fraga \b0\par\par\uc2 1,504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2001\par 16:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Faced with a dramatic weakening of the real in recent weeks, Brazil said Thursday it will drawn down $2 billion in aid funds from the International Monetary Fund while also holding on to an existing $1.8 billion loan. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Arminio Fraga said the decision aims at reinforcing the Brazilian balance of payments, which is current under pressure amid an energy crisis, turmoil in neighboring Argentina and local political jitters. \par \par \uc2  The announcement comes less than 14 hours after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy committee decided to raise interest rates by 150 basis points to 18.25%, the biggest increase since March 1999. Economists were expecting a 50-basis-point hike. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said such problems are "transitory," and Brazil is just improving its safety margin to go through this "transition phase." \par \par \uc2  He said the government will unveil in coming days a set of measures to boost the energy supply and foster exports. \par \par \uc2  No new measures regarding \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were announced, as many market players were expecting earlier Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market whenever it considers necessary. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real has lost more than 20% of its value since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Brazil received a $41.5 billion aid package from the IMF and other international lenders in late 1998, to help South America's biggest nation weather world financial turmoil. A year ago, Brazil said the crisis was over and decided to pay down nearly all the loans it had drawn down. \par \par \uc2  This IMF agreement expires in November, and government officials have said they don't intend to renew this accord. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said Brazil will raise a total $7 billion on international capital markets this year, up from an initial forecast of $6 billion, to help finance the balance of payments. \par \par \uc2  Additionally, he said a total $3.8 billion in foreign capital will enter the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The funds come from the sale of state-owned assets and bond sales by the National Development Bank, or BNDES, and Petroleo Brasileiro SA (PBR). \par \par \uc2  He said these measures will help Brazil close the year with international reserves of some $37 billion, up from $36.3 billion this week. \par \par \uc2  Fraga's comments helped lift the real to as strong as BRR2.390 to the dollar - its best level since June 12. \par \par \uc2  Before Fraga started speaking the real was trading at around the BRR2.424 mark. Earlier Thursday, the real opened at BRR2.454 after closing Wednesday at BRR2.468 following a larger-than-expected 150 basis points hike in interest rates to 18.25%. \par \par \uc2  During the session so far, the real has gained as much as 3.3%. By 1452 GMT, the real was trading a little weaker compared to the intraday high at a midrate of BRR2.413 to the dollar. Over the year to date, the real has depreciated as much as 21%. \par \par \uc2  The comments also helped lift stocks which were largely trading flat at the start of the session. The main Sao Paulo index was up 1.1% at 14,730 points on volume of BRR177 million. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the Treasury will no longer buy dollars on the market to honor foreign debt payments. \par \par \uc2  Under a plan unveiled at the beginning of the year, the government would buy up to $1.2 billion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market instead of using international reserves. The measure was among the first factors putting pressure on the real in early 2001. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, reserves will get a boost from the sale of some state assets. \par \par \uc2  Fraga said the government expects to net $900 million from the sale of a stake in Petrobras. A stake sale in CVRD - expected near the end of the year - will net the government about $1.2 billion, Fraga added. \par \par \uc2  This is part of the planned 2001 sale of about $4.9 billion of stock held by the BNDES in local companies. \par \par \uc2  Stock sales of about $3 billion in mining giant Companhia Vale do Rio Doce SA (RIO) and $1 billion in shares of oil giant Petrobras were planned. \par \par \uc2  Participation in the $1 billion February sale of insurer Cia Nacional de Seguros Gerais, or Sasse, should give the government some $300 million, Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the government and BNDES participated in a $750 million secondary stock offering by aircraft maker Empresa Brasileira de Aeronautica SA (ERJ), or Embraer. \par \par \uc2  On the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  front, Fraga said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent $445 million from international reserves to prop up the sagging real on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the government will also move to lift the controversial PIS and Cofins taxes to make exports more competitive. \par \par \uc2  Unlike a value added tax, the two taxes apply to revenues from corporations at all stages of the production process, leading to double taxation. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor also said the government is studying changes to the financial movements tax, or CPMF, for capital markets transactions. The CPMF, currently at 0.38%, applies to everything from checking account deposits to stock purchases. \par \par \uc2  The potential tax cuts - along with measures to boost energy supply and exports - are part of an attempt by the government to create a better "investment environment," for private companies, Fraga said. \par \par \uc2  In total, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects that the additional bond sales unveiled Thursday, plus the IMF loan and other credit lines from multilateral lenders, will reinforce reserves by $6.2 billion by year-end. \par \par \uc2  At the same time, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects to see a total inflow of $4.6 billion into the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by the end of 2001. \par \par \uc2  The BNDES is expected to raise $400 million on international capital markets - funds which will go to the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The additional $1 billion in sovereign debt planned this year will go to reserves. \par \par \uc2  From multilateral lenders excluding the IMF, Brazil plans to get $800 million to boost reserves while another $1 billion loan would come through the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  Of the $3.8 billion in sales of state assets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sees $600 million going to reserves. \par \par \uc2  The $2 billion IMF tranche will also reinforce the country's stock of hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Fraga said Wednesday's interest-rate hike, the set of "topical" measures unveiled Thursday, along with the "solid fundamentals of our economy" will help Brazil weather the current turbulence. \par \par \uc2  "Our response is firm and clear, and we believe there's room to reverse this (current) situation," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will unveil a detailed forecast for the country's balance of payments financing needs next week, said Fraga. \par \par \uc2  Fraga later reiterated that the decision to draw down the additional $2 billion IMF tranche and not settle the outstanding $1.8 billion IMF loan ahead of schedule doesn't mean Brazil is seeking to renew the IMF agreement. \par \par \uc2  He said these loans are medium-term credit lines that expire in three to five years. \par \par \uc2  Fraga also said the government would unveil measures to tighten the books of federal banks over the next few days. \par \par \uc2  Such measures will look to improve corporate governance and transparency of banks such as Banco do Brasil SA (E.BDB) - the nation's biggest bank in terms of total assets. \par \par \uc2  The government is currently putting the final touches to a federal bank restructuring program to rescue capital-deficient banks. As a part of this, the program will also recapitalize the banks. \par \par \uc2  Fraga declined to elaborate on the plan. \par \par \uc2  Robert Lacoursiere, banking analyst at Lehman Brothers in New York, welcomed the move, saying it would "infuse more rationality in the manner that government-backed banks compete." \par \par \uc2  Lacoursiere, who recently talked to Banco do Brasil, said: "Management indicated that they have been pushing for compliance with government capital requirements and expect to do so by as early as the end of the second quarter, implying that an announcement by the end of next week for a solution for its capital deficit of approximately BRR2.7 billion may be forthcoming." \par \par \uc2  He said management wouldn't comment on the exact mechanism that would resolve the problem but added that a likely option could involve the issuance of subordinated debt combined with a swap of approximately BRR2.7 billion, or a portion of it, of 20-year, zero-coupon non-tradable government paper. \par \par \uc2  According to O Estado de S. Paulo Thursday, the government is looking at the possibility of issuing more than BRR80 billion worth of debt to rescue the federal banks. The government is studying ways of using special financial mechanisms to avoid putting pressure on the handling of state debt and not hurt surpluses that are expected to contribute to the consolidated public sector primary surplus going forward. \par \par \uc2  By the end of March, Banco do Brasil had BRR143.4 billion in total assets. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Arai, Terry Wade and Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3145-1478; brazil@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx6l02pqe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
