\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil Ctrl Bk Calls Emergency Dollar-Indexed Bond Sale \b0\par\par\uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2001\par 20:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones Energy Service \par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help prop up the weakening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by holding an emergency sale of up to 1 billion reals ($1=BRR2.3600) in dollar-indexed debt. \par \par \uc2  The move to offer debt comes as the real depreciated to BRR2.368 to the dollar - its weakest-ever point in its seven-year history. The debt offer began at 1820 GMT and will finish at 1850 GMT. \par \par \uc2  By 1840 GMT, the real improved and was trading at BRR2.360. The previous all-time intraday low was BRR2.348 set on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the auction, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still looks set to close at another all-time low. Wednesday, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished at a midrate of BRR2.345 to the dollar, its weakest point since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was first introduced in 1994 to stamp out inflation. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last offered dollar-indexed debt on April 20 but failed to sell all the bonds on offer. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  then managed to sell just BRR1.06 billion, or 53%, of the BRR2 billion batch of 7-month dollar-pegged bonds on offer. The yield was 13.6%, up from 8.9% on 5-month bonds sold earlier the same week. \par \par \uc2  The real is declining primarily on Argentina's announcement on its much-awaited debt swap. \par \par \uc2  While the Argentine government Thursday said it would offer four new bonds - three dollar-denominated and one peso-denominated - and that the offer expires June 1, with the results being released June 4, the market here remained disappointed with the level of disclosure. \par \par \uc2  Traders blamed the decline on the real largely because the Argentine government didn't provide any estimate on how large the swap will be. While speculation pegs the size at around $20 billion, traders said the market was worried that there wouldn't be enough investor support for such a swap. \par \par \uc2  "The Argentine government's silence on this point has the market worried here, and it looks like we are going to have to wait a bit longer before the market gets the details it wants, possibly another week," said a Sao Paulo-based trader. \par \par \uc2  Traders also pointed to Brazil's current energy crisis, saying that a government plan to introduce rationing at the start of June was doing much damage to the real as the market is uncertain as to how much such a move will hurt the local economy. \par \par \uc2  "The energy crisis has taken the real to new lows over the last week, it hasn't gone away and the worst is about to come," said a Rio de Janeiro-based trader. \par \par \uc2  The government energy rationing plan will finish in December. Brasilia has called on industry, commerce and consumers to cut consumption by 20% to try and conserve depleted water reservoir levels. More than 90% of electricity comes from hydroelectric plants. \par \par \uc2  -By Anthony Dovkants and Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11-3813-1988; brazil@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument nrg0000020010712dx5o00qm5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
