\par\par \b \uc2  Brazil's Real Opens At BRR2.2965/Dlr Vs BRR2.266 End Tue \b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2001\par 14:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- The Brazilian real opened at an all-time intraday low Wednesday, dragged down by mounting concerns about neighboring Argentina and local political jitters. \par \par \uc2  The real opened at midrate of BRR2.2965 on a wide BRR2.294-BRR2.299 spread. At around 1300 GMT, the real had crossed the key psychological barrier of BRR2.30 to the dollar, being quoted at a midrate of BRR2.3015 on a BRR2.300-BRR2.3030 spread. \par \par \uc2  Tuesday the real finished at BRR2.266, a historic closing low. \par \par \uc2  The slide follows fears that Argentine President Fernando de la Rua will face problems in the widely-expected process to replace Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and concern over further noise on the local political front. \par \par \uc2  "There are rumors that (Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Pedro) Pou will take legal action to stay in his position," said a Sao Paulo trader. \par \par \uc2  The trader added that on the Brazilian political front, the odds are growing that Brazilian legislators will start a congressional inquiry committee to look into allegations of high-level government corruption. \par \par \uc2  Expectations that de la Rua would fire Pou helped Brazilian and Argentine equity markets rebound Tuesday. Pou and Economy Minister Domingo Cavallo disagreed over certain measures to revive their nation's struggling economy. \par \par \uc2  At around 1330 GMT, the real was changing hands at a midrate of BRR2.3065 to the dollar, off an earlier intraday low of BRR2.31 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  A trader with a major foreign bank said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't expected to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market by selling dollar-indexed bonds as it did last week because the chances that its action will be effective are very little. \par \par \uc2  "Banks have dollars coming out of their ears so there's no demand for hedge" against \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, said the trader. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to either enter the market directly selling dollars to investors or nothing will work," he said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a floating exchange rate regime and usually avoids \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly on the market unless volatility is high amid thin liquidity, when market movements decouple from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  -By Adriana Arai, Dow Jones Newswires; 55-11 3813-1988 adriana.arai@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1156 1843 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4p01vls\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
