\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Intervenes \highlight\uc2  In  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  To Fight Argentine Contagion \b0\par\par\uc2 960 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 02:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SAO PAULO -(Dow Jones)- Brazil is scrambling to keep two years of hard-fought stability from going down the tubes amid growing fears of Argentine contagion. \par \par \uc2  For the second time in three sessions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to finish at its weakest mark since it was introduced in 1994. \par \par \uc2  Investors are dumping reals on expectations that Brazil's neighbor to the south is on the brink of collapse. Many believe Argentine President Fernando de la Rua won't have enough political support to carry out Friday's announced plan to cut spending by $4.45 billion over the next two years, in the latest bid to reduce the fiscal deficit and regain investor confidence. \par \par \uc2  Ultimately, the fear is that Argentina may have to restructure its debt or devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is pegged at one to one to the dollar through a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. And that's largely why the Brazilian real has lost as much as 5.9% of its value since the start of March, say analysts. \par \par \uc2  "Investors are looking for protection in light of a possible contagion from a crisis in Argentina," said Fabio Akira, a senior economist at BBV Banco in Sao Paulo. "If there were an economic shock in Argentina, all emerging markets worldwide would suffer." \par \par \uc2  Argentine contagion fears build on a cluster of existing negative factors already hurting the real, including the U.S. economic slowdown, a widening trade deficit and bickering within President Fernando Henrique Cardoso's ruling coalition. \par \par \uc2  The real, which closed 2000 at BRR1.95 to the dollar, had already weakened to BRR2.047 by the end of February. But losses have mounted in the past week as Argentine sentiment turns south again, with the real sinking to a new 24-month low of BRR2.1760 in intraday activity Monday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the recent decline of the real has little to do with Brazil's domestic economic outlook, which remains largely healthy amid 4% to 5% growth forecasts. \par \par \uc2  But a persistently weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can contaminate healthy fundamentals. Such a trend may compromise the government's 4% inflation target this year, increase the country's debt burden and force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates for the first time since March 1999. \par \par \uc2  After burning through an undisclosed amount of dollars to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  opted to flood the market with short-term dollar indexed bonds Monday. \par \par \uc2  While direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday did little to arrest the real's decline, the two emergency bond sales late Monday injected the equivalent of some $500 million into the market, helping the real finish on a stronger note for the first time in six consecutive sessions. It ended Monday at BRR2.1190 to the dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Seeks To Preserve Economy On Two Fronts\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the fiscal front, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's depreciation could make Brazilian\par accounts look fragile again despite a two-year track record of austerity\par efforts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The immediate impact would be on the country's debt burden, as around\par one-fifth of Brazil's BRR554-billion in domestic debt is linked to the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate. There's also a smaller effect on Brazil's net\par foreign debt of BRR102 billion, as the government only factors in the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  depreciation on payments being made in the current month rather\par than on the whole stock of debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A first sign of deterioration may be seen next week, when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  releases public accounts for February.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's a big risk that the debt ratio (to gross domestic product)\par rose in February, which would raise a red flag and questions about\par Brazil's fiscal health," said Raul Velloso, an economist and former\par government official based in Brasilia.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The other concern is inflation.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After successfully meeting its goal of reducing inflation to 6% in\par 2000, the Brazilian government is aiming to further quell the rate to 4%\par by the end of 2001.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, such a target could be complicated by a weakening real as\par local companies - which are starting to face extra costs for imported\par materials and equipment - become increasingly anxious to pass along\par higher prices to costumers. While companies weren't able to do this over\par the past couple of years, analysts say that this pattern may be changing\par as consumer purchasing power grows amid wage hikes and more accessible\par credit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   Real Taking Cues From 1-Month Argentine Peso Forward\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Over the last few sessions, Brazil's real has been tracking the\par direction of the one-month forward contract on Argentina's peso,\par according to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analysts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Premiums on peso forwards - which investors use to hedge against any\par change in Argentina's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy when they buy peso-denominated\par bonds - have risen about five-fold in the last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The level of the one-month peso has shot up dramatically in the last\par few sessions...and this has an immediate effect on the real," said Greg\par Anderson, emerging markets \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist with FleetBoston\par Financial.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The most recent round of Argentine worries is different from the last\par one, which eased when the International Monetary Fund announced a $40\par billion bailout package for Argentina in December.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At that time, Argentina's situation appeared less dire, and it was the\par six-month peso forward that was spiking.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Argentine government officials managed to secure some breathing room\par late last year, after the IMF also agreed to allow the country to\par increase its 2001 fiscal deficit targets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But officials have acknowledged in recent days that they'll overshoot\par the revised first-quarter target by an ample margin.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Adriana Arai, Terry Wade and Anthony Dovkants, Dow Jones\par Newswires; 55-11 3813-1988 brazil@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3k018cj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
